<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cai Lun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 62–121</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cai Lun was a Chinese court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> who, as director of the imperial workshops, presented an improved method of making paper to Emperor He in 105 CE. His paper was made from tree bark, hemp, old rags and fishing nets, cheaper than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and lighter than bamboo strips. Chinese tradition credits him as the inventor of paper, though earlier forms existed, and papermaking later spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-lun.jpg" alt="Cai Lun" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cai-lun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
